WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.614
Three days and three nights had gone by since the train had left San Francisco.

2
00:00:05.615 --> 00:00:13.104
The travelers had passed the Great Salt Lake in Utah, traveled over the Rocky Mountains, and crossed the prairies of Wyoming.

3
00:00:13.105 --> 00:00:20.862
In Nebraska they had reached the Platte River, which the rail line was now following, 350 miles before Omaha.

4
00:00:20.863 --> 00:00:27.089
Four more days and nights remained until they would reach New York. Phileas Fogg was right on schedule.

5
00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:33.585
Mr. Fogg and his friends continued their whist game, and no one complained about the length of the trip.

6
00:00:33.586 --> 00:00:39.367
During the morning Fix had won several guineas, which he now seemed likely to lose.

7
00:00:39.368 --> 00:00:42.530
The train pulled into the station at Plum Creek.

8
00:00:42.531 --> 00:00:49.982
Mr. Fogg was about to play a winning spade when a voice from behind him said, "A smart whist player would play a diamond!"

9
00:00:49.983 --> 00:00:51.907
It was Colonel Proctor.

10
00:00:51.908 --> 00:00:56.423
"I see I have found you, Englishman," said the colonel.

11
00:00:56.424 --> 00:01:00.417
Aouda turned pale. She seized Mr. Fogg's arm.

12
00:01:00.418 --> 00:01:05.669
But it was Mr. Fix who stood up and said, "Pardon me, but this is my affair.

13
00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:13.932
You hit me, Colonel, and now you are being insolent with one of my friends. I will challenge you myself!"

14
00:01:13.933 --> 00:01:18.398
"Mr. Fix, you will not," said Mr. Fogg, rising from his seat.

15
00:01:18.399 --> 00:01:23.739
"The colonel has insulted me a second time by suggesting that I play a diamond.

16
00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:26.936
I have no more patience for this rude American.

17
00:01:26.937 --> 00:01:29.783
I challenge you to a duel, Colonel!"

18
00:01:29.784 --> 00:01:36.206
"I accept your challenge," replied Colonel Proctor, "with whatever weapon you choose!"

19
00:01:36.207 --> 00:01:42.661
Passepartout wanted to beg Mr. Fogg not to proceed but knew that, as Fogg's servant, he must hold his tongue. Aouda,

20
00:01:42.662 --> 00:01:47.147
on the other hand, pleaded with Mr. Fogg not to risk his life. Mr.

21
00:01:47.148 --> 00:01:54.102
Fogg calmly selected four revolvers from Passepartout's collection and handed two to the colonel. He

22
00:01:54.103 --> 00:01:58.355
left the car, and the American followed him out onto the platform. The

23
00:01:58.356 --> 00:02:02.730
weather had turned cold and scattered snowflakes were drifting in the air. The

24
00:02:02.731 --> 00:02:07.220
conductor hurried to them to say the train was about to get underway again. "We

25
00:02:07.221 --> 00:02:12.999
are already twenty minutes late, gentlemen. Please board the train," he said. "We

26
00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:18.950
mean to fight a duel! And we would like to do it here and now," said Mr. Fogg. "I'm

27
00:02:18.951 --> 00:02:21.988
really very sorry," said the conductor. "Under

28
00:02:21.989 --> 00:02:26.999
any other circumstances, I'd be willing to oblige you, but there is no time. Why

29
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:30.252
not fight as we go along?" The

30
00:02:30.253 --> 00:02:37.460
men reboarded the train. As it left the station, they followed the conductor to the last car. The

31
00:02:37.461 --> 00:02:40.918
conductor quickly relocated its few passengers. Mr.

32
00:02:40.919 --> 00:02:48.849
Fogg and Colonel Proctor, each carrying two six-barreled revolvers, faced each other at opposite ends of the empty car. They

33
00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:52.737
agreed to begin firing on the count of three. Fix

34
00:02:52.738 --> 00:02:57.374
and Passepartout, their hearts beating, waited outside the door. Suddenly

35
00:02:57.375 --> 00:03:04.671
they heard savage cries, accompanied by gunshots, but the shots did not come from the last car of the train. Shrieks

36
00:03:04.672 --> 00:03:08.468
of terror came from the passengers. Colonel

37
00:03:08.469 --> 00:03:16.355
Proctor and Mr. Fogg, revolvers in hand, left their dueling car and rushed to the forward cars with Fix and Passepartout. They

38
00:03:16.356 --> 00:03:21.255
saw right away what was causing the commotion. A

39
00:03:21.256 --> 00:03:26.259
hundred Indians had leaped from galloping horses onto the train. Three

40
00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:30.315
had climbed onto the engine and knocked the engineer unconscious. Now

41
00:03:30.316 --> 00:03:36.515
the train was racing forward at an uncontrollable speed. The Indians were armed with guns. They

42
00:03:36.516 --> 00:03:41.104
slipped through the windows and doors of the cars, shooting at the passengers. Many

43
00:03:41.105 --> 00:03:45.732
passengers were armed as well and fired back. The

44
00:03:45.733 --> 00:03:48.792
conductor was fighting beside Mr. Fogg. He

45
00:03:48.793 --> 00:03:54.878
knew that a garrison of soldiers was at the next station, Fort Kearney, which they were fast approaching. Their

46
00:03:54.879 --> 00:03:59.438
only hope was to stop the train so that the soldiers could come to their aid. A

47
00:03:59.439 --> 00:04:02.648
shot hit the conductor in the chest. As

48
00:04:02.649 --> 00:04:09.328
he fell, he cried, "Unless the train is stopped, we are lost!" "It

49
00:04:09.329 --> 00:04:14.653
shall be stopped," said Phileas Fogg, preparing to rush through the cars to the engine. "Stay,

50
00:04:14.654 --> 00:04:19.662
monsieur, and protect Aouda," said Passepartout. "I will go!" Mr.

51
00:04:19.663 --> 00:04:27.782
Fogg had no time to stop the courageous Passepartout, who crawled through a door and slipped under the train! While

52
00:04:27.783 --> 00:04:32.815
bullets whizzed between the Indians and the passengers, smashing windows and puncturing seats, Passepartout

53
00:04:32.816 --> 00:04:36.828
clung dangerously above the speeding tracks. He

54
00:04:36.829 --> 00:04:41.805
crawled forward from one car to the next until he reached the first car. Hanging

55
00:04:41.806 --> 00:04:48.437
by one hand, he released the chains that attached the engine to the rest of the train. The

56
00:04:48.438 --> 00:04:53.790
train shuddered violently, and the engine detached from the rest of the cars. The engine

57
00:04:53.791 --> 00:04:57.903
raced ahead, but the cars began to slow. After several

58
00:04:57.904 --> 00:05:03.696
minutes they stopped, only a hundred feet from the station. Passepartout crawled

59
00:05:03.697 --> 00:05:07.136
out from underneath the first car. Before he

60
00:05:07.137 --> 00:05:11.322
could brush himself off, he felt a pistol against his head. He turned

61
00:05:11.323 --> 00:05:20.207
to see a group of Indians had surrounded him on horseback, and everything went black. Passepartout's Guidebook

62
00:05:20.208 --> 00:05:23.036
Help! Help! Somebody, help me!

63
00:05:23.037 --> 00:05:27.863
Oh, hello there. As you can see, I'm in a bit of trouble, captured by Indians!

64
00:05:27.864 --> 00:05:30.829
Let's hope Mr. Fogg comes to my rescue soon.

65
00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:35.438
At least he didn't get killed in that duel. What was he thinking?

66
00:05:35.439 --> 00:05:40.335
Perhaps you have realized this on your own, but a duel is a confrontation between two men.

67
00:05:40.336 --> 00:05:45.116
A duel is usually arranged when one man is insulted or wronged by the other man.

68
00:05:45.117 --> 00:05:51.006
For example, Mr. Fogg said that Colonel Proctor had insulted him twice, so he challenged Proctor to a duel.

69
00:05:51.007 --> 00:05:54.338
It was a way of regaining his honor.

70
00:05:54.339 --> 00:05:58.784
Duels are usually fought with guns and are common in Europe and the United States.

71
00:05:58.785 --> 00:06:04.544
They frequently take place at dawn so the men will not be interrupted by townspeople or police.

72
00:06:04.545 --> 00:06:11.887
Duels are also common among cowboys in the "Wild West" of America, especially when a new cowboy comes to town.

73
00:06:11.888 --> 00:06:17.772
For a duel with guns, two men stand back-to-back, and then walk forward a certain number of steps.

74
00:06:17.773 --> 00:06:21.801
After that, they quickly turn to face each other and shoot.

75
00:06:21.802 --> 00:06:27.914
It's a dangerous way to settle an argument!

